Lichen Planus (Mild Oral Reticular)
Autoimmune Conditions Mild Severity
Wickham striae inside cheeks, asymptomatic, discovered during routine dental exams

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AMS-023
Reason for Visit: Acute severe health anxiety and subjective throat tightness triggered by the sudden discovery of oral mucosal lesions.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated urgent assessment was required to evaluate acute oral mucosal changes associated with severe anxiety and subjective dysphagia. Immediate clinical evaluation was required to exclude acute oral candidiasis, leukoplakia with impending airway compromise, or a severe allergic stomatitis prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I was brushing my teeth and saw these white webs all over the inside of my cheeks. I googled it, and I'm having a panic attack because it looks exactly like mouth cancer.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports painless white lacy streaks on buccal mucosa. The lesions were entirely asymptomatic and likely present for months, but were suddenly noticed by the patient in the mirror two hours ago. This discovery triggered an acute, severe panic attack characterized by palpitations, hyperventilation, and a subjective feeling that the throat was closing over. Patient has been compulsively checking the mouth with a flashlight.
Functional Status: Independent
Social/Safety Concerns: High acute health anxiety driving physiological distress.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/88 | HR: 110 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 22 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 72 Kg
General: Alert, visibly panicking, hyperventilating, holding a mirror and flashlight.
Physical Exam: Oropharyngeal exam reveals bilateral, symmetric, painless white lacy streaks (Wickham striae inside cheeks, asymptomatic, discovered during routine dental exams usually). The mucosa is intact without ulceration, induration, or erythematous borders. No plaques scrape off. Tonsils are normal. Uvula is midline. Neck exam shows no cervical lymphadenopathy. Airway is completely patent. Cardiovascular exam shows sinus tachycardia secondary to anxiety.
Environmental Observations: Bathroom sink covered in mouthwashes and mirrors.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. No current medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Severe Health Anxiety triggered by incidental Lichen Planus (Mild Oral Reticular) - Status: Stable oral findings, resolving anxiety
Differentials:

Oral Candidiasis (Thrush) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The white streaks are incorporated into the mucosa and do not scrape off with a tongue depressor, and the patient is completely asymptomatic to eating/drinking.

Oral Squamous Cell Carcinoma (Leukoplakia) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Bilateral, symmetric, lacy pattern rather than a unilateral, thick, indurated plaque.

Acute Allergic Stomatitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Complete absence of mucosal swelling, erythema, lip angioedema, or pain.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute autonomic arousal and subjective airway tightness secondary to discovering a new oral lesion. Triage appropriately flagged the case for physical assessment to rule out acute airway edema or severe infectious stomatitis. Clinical examination definitively established a completely patent airway and identified the lesions as classic, benign, reticular oral Lichen Planus. The immediate clinical differentiation between a benign autoimmune finding and a suspected malignancy/anaphylaxis safely and rapidly de-escalated a severe panic attack, entirely satisfying the need for urgent after-hours medical intervention.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Reassurance provided regarding the benign, non-cancerous nature of the finding. Instructed on box-breathing to resolve hyperventilation.
Positive Behavioral Change: Put away the flashlight and mirror, and refuse to check your mouth for the next 24 hours. By intentionally stopping the compulsive checking behavior, you will drastically lower your adrenaline levels and prove to yourself that the condition is harmless and unchanging.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Routine dental or GP appointment next week for confirmation and baseline photography.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the nearest Emergency Department immediately if you develop actual difficulty swallowing liquids, sudden severe swelling of your lips or tongue, or stridor (a high-pitched whistling sound when breathing).

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: Reticular oral lichen planus is a harmless autoimmune variation of the mouth lining. It does not turn into cancer overnight and often requires no treatment whatsoever if painless.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek medical review if the white lines become painful, red, or develop into open, bleeding ulcers.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The white web-like pattern is a mild immune reaction that has likely been there for a long time. It is not an infection and it is not cancer. The tight feeling in your throat was pure muscle tension from panic.
Actionable Steps: Stop using harsh mouthwashes immediately, as these can irritate the mild inflammation. Rest tonight and do not look in your mouth again.

SMS INFORMATION

Dx: Oral Lichen Planus (benign white lines). Exam confirms NO cancer, NO infection, and airway is completely safe. Throat tightness is anxiety. See GP next week.

SMS EDUCATION

Stop checking your mouth with a light. Use slow, deep breaths to calm your heart rate. Go to ED only if your lips swell or you cannot swallow water.
